Task 1: Establishing Baselines and Detecting Resource Bottlenecks
Step 1.1: Define Performance Tuning Objectives
Performance tuning is the systematic process of optimizing system resources to achieve better performance, efficiency, and user experience. The primary goals are:

Maximize Throughput â�� increase work completed per unit of time
Minimize Response Time â�� reduce the time between request and response
Optimize Resource Utilization â�� efficiently use CPU, memory, disk, and network resources
Improve Scalability â�� enable systems to handle increased load effectively
Enhance User Experience â�� provide consistent, responsive system behavior
Reduce Costs â�� optimize resource usage to minimize hardware and operational expense
Key performance metrics you will use throughout this lab: CPU utilization, load average, memory usage/swap, disk I/O throughput and wait time, and process-level resource consumption.

Step 1.2: Establish a Baseline Performance Report
Create a baseline monitoring script:

cat > ~/performance_baseline.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== SYSTEM PERFORMANCE BASELINE ==="
echo "Date: $(date)"
echo ""

echo "=== CPU Information ==="
lscpu | grep -E "Model name|CPU\(s\)|Thread|Core"
echo ""

echo "=== Memory Information ==="
free -h
echo ""

echo "=== Disk Information ==="
df -h
echo ""

echo "=== Current Load Average ==="
uptime
echo ""

echo "=== Top 5 CPU Consuming Processes ==="
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -6
echo ""

echo "=== Top 5 Memory Consuming Processes ==="
