Troubleshooting
If detect_cpu_bottleneck.sh or detect_memory_bottleneck.sh reports a false "WARNING" on an otherwise idle system, what other processes or scheduled jobs might be consuming resources in the background, and which command would you use to identify them?
If ~/optimize_disk.sh reports that the scheduler file does not exist for your block device, what does that imply about the underlying storage (physical disk vs. virtualized/cloud block storage), and how would you confirm which storage backend your lab environment is actually using?
Conclusion
In this lab, you moved through the full performance tuning workflow: establishing a system baseline, building detection scripts to identify CPU, memory, and disk bottlenecks, applying concrete tuning changes such as CPU governor selection, process nice values, CPU affinity, virtual memory sysctl parameters, and disk scheduler/read-ahead settings, and finally validating all of it with a comprehensive, repeatable performance test suite that produces timestamped logs and a summary report. The key takeaway is that performance tuning is not a one-time action but an iterative measure-tune-verify cycle, where every change must be backed by before-and-after data rather than assumption. For system administrators, the best practice demonstrated here is to always baseline first, change one variable at a time, and keep automated, reusable tooling so that tuning decisions can be reproduced and audited later. As a next step, consider extending these scripts to monitor network I/O bottlenecks, integrating them with a persistent time-series monitoring stack such as Prometheus and Grafana, and exploring kernel-level tuning through tuned profiles for workload-specific optimization. For further study, consult the Red Hat Performance Tuning Guide, the man pages for sysctl, tune2fs, and iostat, and the official stress-ng documentation for advanced workload simulation options.
